WEBVTT

00:00.720 --> 00:02.440
This right over here
is a self-portrait

00:02.440 --> 00:04.520
that Rembrandt made
in 1640, and what's

00:04.520 --> 00:07.300
really interesting about it
is like other great artists

00:07.300 --> 00:09.370
like Leonardo da Vinci
and Salvador Dali

00:09.370 --> 00:12.090
and many, many, many
others, Rembrandt really

00:12.090 --> 00:15.980
cared about something
called the golden ratio.

00:15.980 --> 00:18.320
And I've done whole
videos about it.

00:18.320 --> 00:21.440
And it's this fascinating,
fascinating, fascinating number

00:21.440 --> 00:27.195
that's usually denoted
by the Greek letter phi.

00:27.195 --> 00:28.570
And if you were
to expand it out,

00:28.570 --> 00:33.990
it's an irrational
number, 1.61803,

00:33.990 --> 00:36.290
and it just goes on
and on and on forever,

00:36.290 --> 00:39.870
but there's some very neat
mathematical properties of phi,

00:39.870 --> 00:41.280
or the golden ratio.

00:41.280 --> 00:46.440
If you start with phi, and
if you were to add to that,

00:46.440 --> 00:47.950
or actually let's
start it this way.

00:47.950 --> 00:54.990
If you were to start with 1
and add to that 1 over phi.

00:54.990 --> 00:56.970
Let me write my phi
a little bit better.

00:56.970 --> 01:01.260
You add to that 1 over
phi, that gives you phi.

01:01.260 --> 01:03.140
So that's kind of a neat thing.

01:03.140 --> 01:06.120
Now, if you were to multiply
both sides of this equation

01:06.120 --> 01:09.740
by phi, you get that,
if you start with phi,

01:09.740 --> 01:12.910
and then if you add 1,
you get phi squared.

01:12.910 --> 01:15.732
So it's a number you add
1, you get its square.

01:15.732 --> 01:17.440
These are all really,
really neat things.

01:17.440 --> 01:19.780
It can even be written
as a continued fraction.

01:19.780 --> 01:29.160
Phi could be rewritten as 1 plus
1 over 1 plus 1 over 1 plus 1

01:29.160 --> 01:31.560
over, and we just go like that
forever and ever and ever.

01:31.560 --> 01:32.819
That also gives you phi.

01:32.819 --> 01:34.860
So hopefully this gives
you a little appreciation

01:34.860 --> 01:37.110
that this is a
really cool number.

01:37.110 --> 01:39.120
And not only is it
cool mathematically,

01:39.120 --> 01:41.179
but it shows up
throughout nature,

01:41.179 --> 01:43.720
and it's something that artists
have cared about because they

01:43.720 --> 01:47.530
believe that it helps
define human beauty.

01:47.530 --> 01:50.060
And we see that Rembrandt
really cared about it

01:50.060 --> 01:51.250
in this painting.

01:51.250 --> 01:52.180
And how can we tell?

01:52.180 --> 01:54.346
Well that's what we're going
to analyze a little bit

01:54.346 --> 01:55.940
through this exercise
in this video.

01:55.940 --> 01:57.360
We can construct a triangle.

01:57.360 --> 01:59.985
Obviously these triangles aren't
part of his original painting.

01:59.985 --> 02:01.460
We superimposed these.

02:01.460 --> 02:03.700
But if you were to put a
base of a triangle right

02:03.700 --> 02:05.790
where his arm is
resting, and then

02:05.790 --> 02:08.139
if you were to have the
two sides of the triangle

02:08.139 --> 02:12.050
outline his arms and shoulders
and then meet at the tip right

02:12.050 --> 02:16.320
at the top of this arch, you
would construct triangle ABD

02:16.320 --> 02:17.930
just like we have here.

02:17.930 --> 02:19.570
And then if you were
to go to his eyes,

02:19.570 --> 02:20.940
and you could imagine
human eyes are

02:20.940 --> 02:23.356
what we naturally look at,
whether we're looking at a face

02:23.356 --> 02:24.720
or a painting of a face.

02:24.720 --> 02:27.530
If you look at his eyes, and if
you were to draw a line there

02:27.530 --> 02:29.930
that's parallel, well, that
really connects the eyes,

02:29.930 --> 02:32.900
and that's parallel to the
BD right over here-- so let's

02:32.900 --> 02:35.890
call that segment PR
right over there--

02:35.890 --> 02:41.710
we'll see that this ratio,
the ratio between this smaller

02:41.710 --> 02:45.770
triangle and this larger
triangle, involves phi.

02:45.770 --> 02:47.330
So this is what we
know, what we're

02:47.330 --> 02:50.390
being told about this painting,
and this is quite fascinating.

02:50.390 --> 02:55.450
The ratio between the length of
segment CD and BC is phi to 1.

02:55.450 --> 02:58.710
So you drop an altitude
from this larger triangle,

02:58.710 --> 03:05.390
this ratio, the ratio of CD, the
length of CD to BC, that's phi.

03:05.390 --> 03:09.240
So clearly, Rembrandt
probably thought about this.

03:09.240 --> 03:11.745
Even more, we know that
PR is parallel to BD.

03:11.745 --> 03:13.370
We've actually
constructed it that way.

03:13.370 --> 03:18.019
So that is going to be parallel
to that right over there.

03:18.019 --> 03:19.560
And so the next clue
is what tells us

03:19.560 --> 03:21.590
that Rembrandt really
thought about this.

03:21.590 --> 03:23.900
The ratio of AC to AQ.

03:23.900 --> 03:27.670
So AC is the altitude
of the larger triangle.

03:27.670 --> 03:31.280
The ratio of that
to AQ, which is

03:31.280 --> 03:35.870
the altitude of this top
triangle, that ratio is phi

03:35.870 --> 03:40.980
plus 1 to 1, or you could even
say that ratio is phi plus 1.

03:40.980 --> 03:43.580
So clearly, Rembrandt
thought a lot about this.

03:43.580 --> 03:45.550
Now using all that
information, let's

03:45.550 --> 03:46.710
just explore a little bit.

03:46.710 --> 03:49.110
Let's see if we can come
up with an expression that

03:49.110 --> 03:51.790
is the ratio of the
area of triangle ABD,

03:51.790 --> 03:53.960
so the area of the
larger triangle,

03:53.960 --> 03:57.080
to the area of triangle APR.

03:57.080 --> 04:00.720
So that's this smaller
triangle right up here.

04:00.720 --> 04:04.330
So we want to find the ratio of
the area of the larger triangle

04:04.330 --> 04:08.080
to the area of the
smaller triangle,

04:08.080 --> 04:10.530
and I want to see if we
can do it in terms of phi,

04:10.530 --> 04:12.760
if we can come up with some
expression here that only

04:12.760 --> 04:17.579
involves phi, or
constant numbers,

04:17.579 --> 04:20.250
or manipulating phi in some way.

04:20.250 --> 04:24.000
So I encourage you to pause the
video now and try to do that.

04:24.000 --> 04:25.300
So let's take it step by step.

04:25.300 --> 04:26.610
What is the area of a triangle?

04:26.610 --> 04:29.820
Well, the area of any triangle
is 1/2 times base times height.

04:29.820 --> 04:32.850
So the area of
triangle ABD we could

04:32.850 --> 04:36.500
write as 1/2 times our base.

04:36.500 --> 04:39.240
Our base is the
length of segment BD.

04:39.240 --> 04:41.844
So 1/2 times BD.

04:41.844 --> 04:42.760
And what's our height?

04:42.760 --> 04:45.030
Well that's the
length of segment AC.

04:45.030 --> 04:48.067
1/2 times BD-- Maybe
I'll do segment AC.

04:48.067 --> 04:49.650
Well, let me do it
in the same color--

04:49.650 --> 04:54.660
times the length of segment AC.

04:54.660 --> 04:55.810
Now what's the area?

04:55.810 --> 04:57.680
This is the area
of triangle ABD.

04:57.680 --> 05:00.680
1/2 base times height.

05:00.680 --> 05:03.030
Now what's the area
of triangle APR?

05:03.030 --> 05:07.480
Well, it's going to be 1/2 times
the length of our base, which

05:07.480 --> 05:10.960
is PR, segment PR,
the length of that,

05:10.960 --> 05:13.690
times the height, which is,
the height is segment AQ,

05:13.690 --> 05:15.580
so the length of segment
AQ, we could just

05:15.580 --> 05:17.750
write it like that, times
the length of segment AQ.

05:17.750 --> 05:20.570
So how can we simplify
this a little bit?

05:20.570 --> 05:23.070
Well, we could divide
the 1/2 by the 1/2.

05:23.070 --> 05:24.980
Those two cancel out.

05:24.980 --> 05:26.830
But what else do we know?

05:26.830 --> 05:29.690
Well, they gave us the
ratio between AC and AQ.

05:32.830 --> 05:38.700
The ratio of AC to AQ right
over here is phi plus 1 to 1.

05:38.700 --> 05:40.820
Or we could just say
this is equal to phi.

05:40.820 --> 05:43.250
Or we could say this is
just equal to phi plus 1.

05:43.250 --> 05:44.532
So let me rewrite this.

05:44.532 --> 05:45.990
Actually, let me
write it this way.

05:45.990 --> 05:48.580
This is going to
be equal to-- So we

05:48.580 --> 05:54.570
have the length of segment BD
over the length of segment PR,

05:54.570 --> 05:57.510
and then this part right
over here we can rewrite,

05:57.510 --> 06:00.049
this is equal to
phi plus 1 over 1.

06:00.049 --> 06:01.340
So I'll just write it that way.

06:01.340 --> 06:06.520
So times phi plus 1 over 1.

06:06.520 --> 06:08.315
So what's the ratio of BD to PR?

06:12.850 --> 06:16.400
So the ratio of the base of
the larger triangle to the base

06:16.400 --> 06:18.869
of the smaller triangle.

06:18.869 --> 06:20.410
So let's think about
it a little bit.

06:20.410 --> 06:22.750
What might jump out at you
is that the larger triangle

06:22.750 --> 06:25.990
and the smaller triangle, that
they are similar triangles.

06:25.990 --> 06:30.260
They both obviously
have angle A in common,

06:30.260 --> 06:33.640
and since PR is
parallel to BD, we

06:33.640 --> 06:36.940
know that this angle
corresponds to this angle.

06:36.940 --> 06:39.510
So these are going to
be congruent angles.

06:39.510 --> 06:43.570
And we know that this
angle corresponds

06:43.570 --> 06:45.560
to this angle right over here.

06:45.560 --> 06:48.190
So now we have three
correspondingly angles

06:48.190 --> 06:49.350
are congruent.

06:49.350 --> 06:52.050
This is congruent to itself,
which is in both triangles.

06:52.050 --> 06:53.427
This is congruent to this.

06:53.427 --> 06:54.510
This is congruent to that.

06:54.510 --> 06:56.135
You have three
congruent angles, you're

06:56.135 --> 06:58.160
dealing with two
similar triangles.

06:58.160 --> 07:00.510
And what's useful
about similar triangles

07:00.510 --> 07:02.770
are the ratio between
corresponding parts.

07:02.770 --> 07:04.920
Corresponding lengths of
the corresponding parts

07:04.920 --> 07:07.860
of the similar triangles
are going to be the same.

07:07.860 --> 07:09.950
And they gave us
one of those ratios.

07:09.950 --> 07:15.410
They gave us the ratio of the
altitude of the larger triangle

07:15.410 --> 07:18.240
to the altitude of
the smaller triangle.

07:18.240 --> 07:22.860
AC to AQ is phi plus 1 to phi.

07:22.860 --> 07:26.375
But since this is true
for one corresponding part

07:26.375 --> 07:27.750
of the similar
triangles, this is

07:27.750 --> 07:30.820
true for any corresponding
parts of the similar triangle,

07:30.820 --> 07:34.120
that the ratio is going
to be phi plus 1 to 1.

07:34.120 --> 07:40.340
So the ratio of BD, the ratio of
the base of the larger triangle

07:40.340 --> 07:42.230
to the base of the
smaller one, that's

07:42.230 --> 07:44.870
also going to be
phi plus 1 over 1.

07:50.200 --> 07:51.450
Let me just write it this way.

07:51.450 --> 07:56.040
This could also be rewritten
as phi plus 1 over 1.

07:56.040 --> 07:57.750
So what does this simplify to?

07:57.750 --> 08:00.740
Well, we have phi plus 1 over
1 times phi plus 1 over 1.

08:00.740 --> 08:02.220
Well, we could just divide by 1.

08:02.220 --> 08:03.470
You're not changing the value.

08:03.470 --> 08:04.900
This is just going
to be equal to,

08:04.900 --> 08:06.620
and we deserve a drum roll now.

08:06.620 --> 08:11.250
This is equal to
phi plus 1 squared.

08:11.250 --> 08:12.319
So that was pretty neat.

08:12.319 --> 08:14.610
And I encourage you to even
think about this because we

08:14.610 --> 08:16.880
already saw that phi plus
1 is equal to phi squared,

08:16.880 --> 08:19.220
and there's all sorts of
weird, interesting ways

08:19.220 --> 08:22.220
you could continue
to analyze this.
